@page "/learn/server-initiated-circuit-pause"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Pause circuits from the server · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Pause circuits from the server</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">You need to restart for a deploy. Can server code ask each tab to save its form first?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Interactive Server · about 5 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> <code>Circuit.RequestCircuitPauseAsync()</code>. Server code can ask a tab to
        pause, and the call returns straight away. Pausing itself isn't new. In .NET 10 a tab could already pause, save
        its <code>[PersistentState]</code> values, and resume later on a new server. What was missing was a clean way
        for the server to ask.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is a <i>New order</i> draft. Each tab runs on a <i>circuit</i>, the session the server keeps for it. The
        draft's customer, product and quantity are <code>[PersistentState]</code>, so they survive a pause. Its button
        stands in for a deploy script: server code asks this tab, and only this tab, to pause.
    </p>
    <p>
        In .NET 10 there was no server method for this, so this version calls the browser's own pause function
        through JS interop.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="Asking through JS interop" Url="demo/server-pause/new-order/net10"
                Label=".NET 10: JS interop" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            Change the <b>Quantity</b> and press <b>Pause this tab from the server</b>. A dialog says <i>The session
            has been paused by the server</i>. Press <b>Resume</b>. The order is still filled in. But you can't see a
            stuck server call on the page, so a red line at the top tells you: the server is still waiting for an
            answer. It always will be.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>The reply needs the connection the pause closes</h2>
    <p>A JS interop call goes to the browser and back over the tab's connection. The pause closes that connection:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">What happens to the awaited call</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">server</span>   await js.InvokeVoidAsync("Blazor.pauseCircuit")  ──▶ <span class="dim">browser</span>
<span class="dim">browser</span>  saves the state, closes the circuit
<span class="dim">browser</span>  the reply ──▶ <span class="err">no connection left to carry it</span>
<span class="dim">server</span>   the await <span class="err">never returns</span>; the code after it never runs</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        If your deploy code awaits each tab in turn, it gets stuck on the first one. You can get around that
        with your own script that calls <code>Blazor.pauseCircuit</code> and returns at once, without waiting. That
        works, but it's JavaScript you have to ship in every app, and it can't tell you whether the tab was still
        there to ask.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Ask through the Circuit</h2>
    <p>
        Blazor hands each tab's <code>Circuit</code> to your circuit handler when the tab connects. Keep it there,
        then call the new method on it. Only the method is new.
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">// A scoped CircuitHandler is handed its circuit when it opens</span>
public override Task OnCircuitOpenedAsync(Circuit circuit, CancellationToken cancel)
{
    Circuit = circuit;
    return Task.CompletedTask;
}

<span class="dim">// Anywhere on the server</span>
<span class="hl">bool asked = await Tracker.Circuit.RequestCircuitPauseAsync();</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        It sends the request to the browser and returns. The browser then pauses just as if it had decided to itself.
        <code>true</code> means the request went out, not that the tab has paused yet. <code>false</code> means it
        didn't ask, because the circuit had closed, hadn't finished starting, or had lost its connection.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same draft, asking through the Circuit" Url="demo/server-pause/new-order"
                Label=".NET 11: RequestCircuitPauseAsync">
        <Caption>
            Do the same: change the <b>Quantity</b>, press <b>Pause this tab from the server</b>, then
            <b>Resume</b>. The order comes back as before. This time the line at the top says the server got
            <code>true</code> back in a few milliseconds, so it can move on to the next tab.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>Both demos share one draft component. A parameter picks how it asks:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/CircuitPause/OrderDraft.razor" Feature="server-initiated-circuit-pause">
        <Caption>
            Three blocks are highlighted; only the last is the fix. The <code>[PersistentState]</code> properties are
            what both demos keep, and they work as they did in .NET 10. The <code>ViaJavaScript</code> branch is
            Fig. 1's call that never returns. The <code>else</code> branch is the new line:
            <code>RequestCircuitPauseAsync</code>.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/CircuitPause/CircuitTracker.cs" Feature="server-initiated-circuit-pause">
        <Caption>
            Where Fig. 2's circuit comes from. Nothing highlighted here is new: circuit handlers have always been given
            their <code>Circuit</code>. Each tab gets its own tracker, never another visitor's.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Restart anyway, or call into the browser</h2>
    <p>We tried each option on .NET 10, with the same order form and a real restart:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Three ways to restart in .NET 10, and what the tab did</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">// no pause: stop the process</span></pre>
                <span class="why">"Rejoining the server…", then "Rejoin failed", then a reload on the new process</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ the form comes back empty</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">await js.InvokeVoidAsync(
    "Blazor.pauseCircuit");</pre>
                <span class="why">the tab pauses and resumes with its data</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ the await never returns</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">// in App.razor, by hand</span>
window.remotePause =
    () =&gt; { Blazor.pauseCircuit(); };</pre>
                <span class="why">called from server code, it returns before the circuit closes; the tab pauses with its data</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ works, but it's a script to ship, and you don't learn whether the tab was asked</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        So the third option does work, but it needs JavaScript you write and ship yourself. And you can't use the
        new method instead: <code>RequestCircuitPauseAsync</code> doesn't exist in .NET 10, so the build fails.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>A server call that returns</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 11 you ask through the <code>Circuit</code>. It's a small gain, but a real one: no script to ship, a
        call that returns, and a <code>bool</code> that tells you if the request went out. Deploy code can ask every
        open tab, then give the browsers a few seconds to save before the process exits. Fig. 2 does this for one tab.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b><code>true</code> isn't "paused".</b> It means the request went out, not that the tab has finished saving. To know when it has, watch for the circuit to close, as Fig. 4's <code>OnCircuitClosedAsync</code> does.</li>
        <li><b>Pausing every tab before a restart?</b> That's the real deploy case. The demo asks one tab only, so it never pauses another visitor. To ask every tab, keep a single list of open circuits: add each one in <code>OnCircuitOpenedAsync</code> and remove it in <code>OnCircuitClosedAsync</code>. When the app starts shutting down (<code>IHostApplicationLifetime.ApplicationStopping</code>), call <code>RequestCircuitPauseAsync</code> on each. Then give the browsers a few seconds to save, with a time limit, before the process exits.</li>
        <li><b>Keep your Data Protection keys.</b> The paused state waits in the browser, encrypted. The new server needs the same keys to read it.</li>
        <li><b>A page can finish saving first.</b> In the browser, Blazor runs any <code>onCircuitPausing</code> callback you've registered before it pauses, so a page can finish its own work first.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
